              }
            ],
            "display": "block",
            "id": "3300940534",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/1F/1F1D4DDF6FE6579118864BA9FB900202/eP-3KBrQ.jpeg",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Gibbs free energy ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is a state function, defined in terms of a system’s enthalpy, entropy, and temperature, that predicts the spontaneity of chemical and physical processes. A negative Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": " value indicates a spontaneous process, while a positive Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": " value indicates a nonspontaneous process. When Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": " is zero, the system is at equilibrium. This concept is crucial for understanding and predicting the behavior of reactions under different conditions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "466566148",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In practical terms, Gibbs free energy is used to determine whether a chemical reaction will proceed without external energy input. For instance, in industrial processes, knowing the value of Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "G"
              },
              {
                "text": " can help engineers design more efficient systems by predicting which reactions will occur naturally. Additionally, in biological systems, Gibbs free energy helps explain how cells perform work, such as muscle contraction and nerve transmission, by harnessing spontaneous reactions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4237778793",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Various methods can be used to determine free energy changes, making Gibbs free energy a versatile tool in both theoretical and applied chemistry. By mastering the concept of Gibbs free energy, you can better understand the driving forces behind chemical reactions and their applications in real-world scenarios."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3427994201",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
